{"qid":"Q5790","names":["Yadava Dynasty"],"title":"Yadava Dynasty","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Yadava (Seuna) dynasty of Devagiri ruled much of the Deccan from their capital at Devagiri (modern Daulatabad, Maharashtra), declaring independence from the declining Western Chalukyas under Bhillama V around 1187. Under Singhana II (r. c. 1200–1246), the kingdom reached its widest extent, stretching from the Narmada in the north to the Tungabhadra in the south, covering present-day Maharashtra, northern Karnataka, and parts of Madhya Pradesh, while subduing the Hoysala, Kakatiya, and Shilahara powers. Alauddin Khalji raided Devagiri in 1296 and extracted tribute; the territory was conquered by Malik Kafur in 1307 and formally annexed to the Delhi Sultanate in 1317 after Singhana III's resistance was crushed. The dynasty was a major patron of Marathi literature, with the poet Hemadri serving as minister and the composer Dnyaneshwar active during this period.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The core Deccan territory is reasonably well documented through inscriptions and Persian sources, but precise frontier boundaries — especially to the south and east — are approximated from campaign records rather than fixed borders.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Yadava dynasty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yadava_dynasty"},{"label":"Britannica: Yadava dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Yadava-dynasty"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Yadava Dynasty","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Dridhaprahara","nativeName":"दृढप्रहार","roman":"Dṛḍhaprahāra","start":860,"end":880,"image